  "hints": [
    "Which traversal visits the tree level by level?",
    "Use a queue (or a list for the current level) and process one level per loop.",
    "Sum the values in the current level, append the sum, then gather all children as the next level."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function levelSums(root) {\n  const sums = []\n  let level = root ? [root] : []\n  while (level.length) {\n    let total = 0\n    const next = []\n    for (const node of level) {\n      total += node.val\n      if (node.left) next.push(node.left)\n      if (node.right) next.push(node.right)\n    }\n    sums.push(total)\n    level = next\n  }\n  return sums\n}\n",
    "py": "def level_sums(root):\n    sums = []\n    level = [root] if root else []\n    while level:\n        sums.append(sum(node[\"val\"] for node in level))\n        level = [child for node in level for child in (node[\"left\"], node[\"right\"]) if child]\n    return sums\n",
    "explanation": "Breadth-first search naturally groups nodes by depth. Each loop handles exactly one level: sum it, then build the next level from its children."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(w)",
    "notes": "A DFS that passes depth down also works, using O(h) stack plus the output list."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "Return the right-side view of the tree (the last node of each level) instead."
  ]
